    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Direct sources like surface rocks, deep mining, and ocean drilling provide limited physical samples of Earth's crust.",
        "Indirect sources, especially seismic waves, gravity, and magnetic surveys, reveal the deep interior structure.",
        "Seismic P-waves travel through solids, liquids, and gases, whereas S-waves travel strictly through solid media.",
        "The Earth consists of three principal layers: the Crust (Oceanic & Continental), Mantle (including Asthenosphere), and Core (Outer & Inner).",
        "Temperature, pressure, and density all progressively increase from the Earth's surface down to its center.",
        "The Asthenosphere in the upper mantle is the primary source of magma for volcanic eruptions."
      ],
      "id": "wx37ck5"
    },
